1. **A slowdown for every caller of `gsFitting`.** In `src/gsModeling/gsFitting.hpp`, the commit adds debug code to the end of `gsFitting<T>::assembleSystem`, which runs on every `compute()`:
   ```cpp
   gsFileData<T> fd;
   gsMatrix<T> C=A_mat.toDense();
   fd << C;
   fd.dump("SlowFittingMatrix");
   ...
   fb.dump("SlowFittingb");
   ```
   This turns the sparse system matrix into a dense `num_basis × num_basis` matrix, so memory and time grow with the square of the basis size. It then writes that matrix as XML to a hard-coded file in the working directory. For a realistic basis (thousands of DOFs) this costs much more than the fit itself and can run out of memory.
   - The same file also adds an assembly-time printout to `compute()` and a flop counter and printout inside the assembly loop, all unconditional.
   - The new `gsFastFitting::assembleSystem` repeats the dense dump (`FastFittingMatrix`, `FastFittingb`).

   This is a performance regression in a core library routine, not just in the example.

2. **An out-of-bounds read in the reviewed example (`fastfitting_surface.cpp`).** `x`, `y` and `z` are only filled when `example` is 1, 2 or 3. The program accepts any value through `-e`, and the final loop always runs:
   ```cpp
   for(index_t i=0; i<uv.cols();i++) { xyz(0,i) = x[i]; ... }
   ```
   With any other value (e.g. `-e 4`), the vectors are empty and `x[i]` reads past their end, which is undefined behaviour.

3. **A smaller cost in `gsFastFitting::compute`.** It calls `A_mat.reservePerColumn(...)`, then `assembleSystem` calls `A_mat.setZero()` before filling the matrix with `A_mat(i,j) +=`. In Eigen, `setZero()` throws away the per-column reservation, so the inserts that follow have to shuffle data around and are slow.

The first problem alone is enough to block the merge: it slows down every existing user of `gsFitting` and leaves leftover debugging that writes files.

The new loop in `gsFupBasis::evalAllDersSingle_into` returns the same value for every evaluation point.

```cpp
T * udata = const_cast<T*>(u.data());
result.resize(n+1, u.cols() );
for ( index_t k = 0; k!=u.cols(); ++k)
    for ( index_t j = 0; j<=n; ++j)
        result(j,k) = __fup_0_16_d_MOD_fupn(&m_p, &anc, udata, &m_knot_length, &j);
```

- **Wrong point passed:** `udata` never moves with the column index `k`. Every call hands Fortran the address of `u(0,0)`. `u` is a 1×N column-major matrix, so the pointer for point `k` should be `udata + k` (or `&u(0,k)`).
- **Effect:** each column of `result` is just the value and derivatives at the first point. The modified example evaluates on a 10-point grid with `gsPointGrid` and prints `val.row(0)` and `val.row(1)` per basis function. Those rows will be one value repeated 10 times instead of the actual function values.

The loop over `k` was added specifically to evaluate many points, so this is a logic error in the added lines, not a problem that was already there.

A smaller issue: the new member `m_knot_length` is never set in the default constructor. The updated `print()` now outputs it, so printing a default-constructed `gsFupBasis` reads an uninitialized value.

