votes.reasoning:
The new zero-argument `toSparseMatrix()` that this commit adds is broken. Its body calls `toSparseMatrix(rvo)`, but the same commit renames the one-argument overload to `toSparseMatrix_into`. So the only `toSparseMatrix` left in the class takes no arguments, and the call `toSparseMatrix(rvo)` cannot resolve: it is a compile error.

```cpp
gsSparseMatrix<T> toSparseMatrix() const
{
    gsSparseMatrix<T> rvo;
    toSparseMatrix(rvo);      // no such overload any more; should be toSparseMatrix_into(rvo)
    return rvo;
}
```

The build only passes today because this member belongs to a class template. Its body is compiled only when something calls it, and a search found no callers yet. I also checked for an explicit instantiation of `gsFiberMatrix`, which would compile every member; there isn't one, only the function-template instantiations in `gsBoehm_.cpp`. Even so, the new public method fails to compile the first time anyone uses it. The fix is to call `toSparseMatrix_into(rvo)` inside it.

